 – Field_3 needs 2 drones. The two that are already moving toward Field_3 (Drone_1 and Drone_2) are the closest and so they are kept on that field.
 – Field_4 needs 3 drones. Looking at the positions (by estimating distances to its center near (40.5,15.5)), the closest available drones (from those originally protecting lower‐threat fields) are Drone_6, Drone_7, and Drone_3.
• With 5 drones now allotted, only 3 drones remain. Between Field_1 (threat 0.09) and Field_2 (threat 0.06), Field_1 is the next most threatened. So we give all remaining drones (Drone_4, Drone_5 and Drone_8) to Field_1 even though full protection there would require four drones.
• Field_2 ends up without any assigned drones because its threat (0.06) is lower than Field_1’s (0.09) and we must use our limited drones to guarantee that the highest‐threat fields are fully protected.

This assignment follows the rule “always fully protect the field with the highest threat level with the closest drones” while assigning each drone to exactly one of the allowed groups.
