Explanation:
• Field_3 has the highest threat level (0.21) and needs 2 drones. Drone_1 and Drone_4 are already protecting Field_3, so we leave them there.
• Field_4 is the next highest threat (0.15) and requires 3 drones. The three drones that are en route (Drone_2, Drone_5, and Drone_8) are assigned to protect Field_4.
• The remaining drones (Drone_3, Drone_6, and Drone_7) are currently protecting Field_1. Although Field_1 is not fully protected (it would need 4 drones), there aren’t extra drones available. Since spreading them or reassigning to Field_2 (threat level 0.03 as well) would still result in only partial protection (and partial coverage is only marginally better than leaving a field unguarded), we keep them on Field_1.
This yields full protection for the most threatened fields, while the lower-threat fields are left with their current (partial or no) coverage.
